---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import Command from '../components/Command.astro'
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro'
import ThemePicker from '../components/ThemePicker.vue'
import { themes } from '../data/catalog'

const bases = themes.filter((theme) => theme.kind === 'base')
const accents = themes.filter((theme) => theme.kind === 'accent')

// The block "ermine theme blue" writes, in the same form as themeBlock() in cli/bin/ermine.mjs.
const blue = themes.find((theme) => theme.name === 'blue')!
const written = `/* ermine theme accent: blue. "ermine theme" replaces this block when you pick another accent.
   To fine-tune a colour, set it again in a rule after this block. */
${blue.css}
/* end ermine theme accent */`
const tweak = `/* After the theme's block, so it wins */
:root {
  --er-primary: light-dark(oklch(0.5 0.22 262), oklch(0.75 0.14 254));
}`
const rose = themes.find((theme) => theme.name === 'rose')!
const scoped = `/* The rose accent for one part of the page only */
${rose.css.replace(':root', '.campaign')}`
---

<Layout title="Themes. Ermine UI" description="Colour themes for Ermine UI: a base that tints the greys and an accent for the primary colour, in light and dark.">
  <div class="wrap doc">
    <header class="doc-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Themes</p>
      <h1 class="headline">Pick a base and an accent.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A base tints the surfaces, text and lines; an accent sets the primary colour and the focus ring. Every pairing is
        checked for readable contrast in light and dark.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ThemePicker client:load themes={themes} />

    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="apply">Using a theme</h2>

      <h3 id="new-project">In a new project</h3>
      <p>Pass the pair to <code>init</code>, and the theme stylesheet it copies starts with them applied.</p>
      <Command args="init --theme slate,blue" />

      <h3 id="existing-project">In a project already set up</h3>
      <p>
        Name a base, an accent, or both. Picking one kind keeps the other, so the second line below keeps the slate base and
        swaps the accent. <code>--reset</code> removes both, back to your stylesheet's own colours. Run <code>theme</code> on
        its own to list the themes and see which ones you are using.
      </p>
      <Command args={['theme slate blue', 'theme rose', 'theme --reset']} />

      <h3 id="what-changes">What changes in your stylesheet</h3>
      <p>
        Each theme goes at the end of your theme stylesheet as a <code>:root</code> block of <code>--er-*</code> variables,
        between two marker comments. The next <code>theme</code> command replaces only what sits between those markers, so
        the rest of the file, including your own edits, stays as it is. This is the block for <code>blue</code>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={written} lang="css" />
      <p>
        Every component reads these variables, so nothing else needs changing. Dark mode works as before: each value holds a
        light and a dark colour in <code>light-dark()</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 id="fine-tune">Adjusting a colour</h3>
      <p>Don't edit inside the markers, because the next <code>theme</code> command replaces that block. Set the variable again in a rule after it:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={tweak} lang="css" />

      <h3 id="by-hand">Without the command line</h3>
      <p>
        Choose a pair in the picker above, open <em>Or paste the CSS</em>, and paste the CSS at the end of your theme stylesheet.
        Put the base before the accent, because both set the primary colour and the later one wins. To theme only one section,
        change <code>:root</code> to a selector for that section:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock code={scoped} lang="css" />

      <h3 id="assistants">From an AI coding tool</h3>
      <p>
        Tools connected to <code>ermine mcp</code> can apply themes with its <code>set_theme</code> tool. Ask for
        "the slate base with a blue accent" and the tool writes the same blocks the command does.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bases">Bases</h2>
      <ul>{bases.map((theme) => <li><code>{theme.name}</code>: {theme.summary}</li>)}</ul>

      <h2 id="accents">Accents</h2>
      <ul>{accents.map((theme) => <li><code>{theme.name}</code>: {theme.summary}</li>)}</ul>
      <p>With no accent, the primary colour is the base's own near-black (near-white in the dark theme).</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
